<<man page: INDEXING AND CROSS-REFERENCE section>>=

@ \fi
The pipeline is accumulated in a single variable, [[filters]], with one
filter command per line, separated by the newline held in [[nl]].  A
filter command may carry arguments and so contain spaces (for example
[[-filter 'elide foo:*']] adds [[elide foo:*]]), but a newline never
occurs inside one, which makes the newline a safe separator and lets
the list hold arbitrarily many filters.  This is why there is no fixed
number of filter \emph{slots}: earlier versions of this script
enumerated a fixed maximum of seven, because a portable [[/bin/sh]]
has no arrays, and a document that stacked enough [[-autodefs]] and
[[-filter]] stages exhausted them with ``ran out of filters''.
Appending a line has no such ceiling.

The append must stay on one source line.  Were the newline written
literally inside the quotes here, noweb would indent the continuation
line to this chunk's reference column and so splice those indentation
spaces into [[filters]]; routing the newline through [[nl]] keeps the
assignment a single line and the variable clean.
<<append [[$newfilter]] to the pipeline>>=
filters="$filters$newfilter$nl"
<<show usage>>=
echo "Usage: $0 [options] [files]" 1>&2
echo "Options recognized are:" 1>&2
<<option printout for usage>>
@
To avoid inserting any extra newlines into the output, 
I use [[@literal]]to insert headers and trailers.
The altered [[PATH]] provides a convenience to people who want to
use simple names to refer to filters in [[$LIB]].
[[markup]] is stage~0 of the failure accounting described below, so
it runs in the same braces as every filter.
<<emit markup on standard output>>=
(
header=
case $wrapper in
  none) ;;
  latex)  header="@header $wrapper $noweboptions" 
	  trailer="@trailer $wrapper" ;;
  *)  header="@header $wrapper$arg" 
      trailer="@trailer $wrapper" ;;
esac
if [ -n "$header" ]; then echo "$header"; fi
PATH="$PATH:$LIB" 
export PATH
<<if verbose, make noise about pipeline>>
<<if verbose, show back end>>
<<build the filter pipeline from [[filters]]>>
if [ -z "$pipeline" ]; then
  { $markup $markopts $arg || echo "0 $?" >>"$statusfile"; }
else
  { $markup $markopts $arg || echo "0 $?" >>"$statusfile"; } | eval "$pipeline"
fi
if [ -n "$trailer" ]; then echo $trailer; fi
)
@
The pipeline is assembled by joining the filter lines with the shell's
[[|]] operator.  Setting [[IFS]] to a newline makes the [[for]] loop
iterate once per filter; newline is one of the shell's whitespace
separators, so the trailing newline left by each append produces no
empty final field.  (The verbose listing restores the normal [[IFS]]
before each [[echo]], so that an empty option variable inside a
filter command---[[$noquote]] in [[finduses $noquote]]---does not
leave a trailing space in the printout, exactly as before.)  The
joined string is run with a single [[eval]],
which both interprets the [[|]] operators and re-tokenizes each
filter's arguments---the same re-tokenization the old per-slot
[[eval $filtera]] performed.

Each stage is also wrapped so that its exit status is not lost.  A
pipeline's status is that of its last command, so a filter that dies
half-way---a missing interpreter, a crash---leaves a truncated
document behind while [[noweave]] still exits~0, and a Makefile
carries on as if the weave had succeeded.  We cannot ask the shell
for the status of every stage (a \texttt{pipefail} option is neither
portable nor, as explained below, quite what we want), so each stage
records its own failure: the command runs inside braces, and if it
exits nonzero, the stage number and the status are appended to a
temporary file.  The command itself appears verbatim inside the
braces, so [[eval]] tokenizes its arguments exactly as before; the
name of the file is left for [[eval]] to expand, so that it need not
be quoted here.
<<build the filter pipeline from [[filters]]>>=
pipeline=
stage=0
oldIFS=$IFS
IFS=$nl
for f in $filters; do
  stage=`expr $stage + 1`
  wrapped="{ $f || echo \"$stage \$?\" >>\"\$statusfile\"; }"
  if [ -z "$pipeline" ]; then
    pipeline="$wrapped"
  else
    pipeline="$pipeline | $wrapped"
  fi
done
IFS=$oldIFS
@
The temporary file is made by noweb's own [[nwmktemp]], as the other
scripts do, once the options are known---so that an early exit for
usage or [[-showautodefs]] creates nothing---and it is removed by a
trap on exit and on the usual signals.
<<create the status file for the pipeline stages>>=
statusfile=$($LIB/nwmktemp) || { echo "$0: Cannot create temporary file" 1>&2; exit 1; }
trap 'rm -f "$statusfile"' 0 1 2 15
@
After the back end has finished we know two things: its own status,
and whatever the stages recorded.  Not every recorded failure is one.
When a stage stops reading its input early---because it has failed,
or because it legitimately ignores the rest---the stages upstream of
it are killed by [[SIGPIPE]] the next time they write, and report
status 141 (128 plus the signal number).  That is a consequence of
what happened downstream, not a failure of its own, so entries with
status 141 are skipped.  (This is why the [[pipefail]] option of
newer shells would be the wrong tool even where it exists: it counts
that [[SIGPIPE]] death as a failure of the whole pipeline.)  Every
other entry is reported by stage number and command---stage~0 is
[[markup]], stage~$n$ the $n$th filter---and the status of the
earliest such stage, the one nearest the cause, becomes [[noweave]]'s
own.  When nothing was recorded, the back end's status is the exit
status, as it always was, now with a message of its own if nonzero.
<<exit with the status of the first failed stage>>=
failedstatus=
if [ -s "$statusfile" ]; then
  oldIFS=$IFS
  IFS=$nl
  for line in `sort -n "$statusfile"`; do
    IFS=$oldIFS
    set -- $line
    stage=$1 status=$2
    if [ "$status" -ne 141 ]; then
      <<name the command of stage [[$stage]] in [[cmd]]>>
      echo "$0: pipeline stage $stage ($cmd) failed with status $status" 1>&2
      if [ -z "$failedstatus" ]; then failedstatus=$status; fi
    fi
    IFS=$nl
  done
  IFS=$oldIFS
fi
if [ -n "$failedstatus" ]; then exit $failedstatus; fi
if [ $backendstatus -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "$0: back end ($backend $noindex $delay $shortxref) failed with status $backendstatus" 1>&2
fi
exit $backendstatus
@
The stage number is turned back into the command it names by picking
the corresponding line of [[filters]].  [[printf]] rather than
[[echo]] copies the lines, since a filter argument may contain
backslashes that some [[echo]]s would interpret.
<<name the command of stage [[$stage]] in [[cmd]]>>=
if [ "$stage" -eq 0 ]; then
  cmd="$markup $markopts $arg"
else
  cmd=`printf '%s\n' "$filters" | sed -n "${stage}p"`
fi
<<if verbose, make noise about pipeline>>=
if [ -n "$verbose" ]; then
  if [ -n "$header" -o -n "$trailer" ]; then
    parenpfx="("
    if [ -n "$header" ]; then echo "(echo $header" 1>&2; parenpfx=" "; fi
    echo "$parenpfx$markup" $markopts $arg 1>&2
    if [ -n "$trailer" ]; then echo " echo $trailer" 1>&2; fi
    echo ") |" 1>&2
  else
    echo $markup $markopts $arg '|' 1>&2
  fi
  oldIFS=$IFS
  IFS=$nl
  for f in $filters; do IFS=$oldIFS; echo $f '|' 1>&2; done
  IFS=$oldIFS
fi
